\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b STUDY SHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b Like rain upon the grass\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Deuteronomy 32:1\u8211?52 | Haftarah: Hosea 14:2\u8211?10; Joel 2:15\u8211?27 | Gospel: Matthew 18:21\u8211?35\par
\f0\fs22\i Moses\u8217? last song begins with the Word coming down like rain and sets the unchanging Rock against a heart that, in abundance, forgets from whom it received everything. Shabbat Shuvah (Hosea, Joel) calls to return \u8212? not to self-made fruit but to its Source \u8212? and Matthew 18 shows where that forgetting or returning becomes visible: in how you treat the other.\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 \u167?0 Observation round\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 0.1 Blind observation\b0\fs24\par


\par The song opens with an image of gift: God's teaching comes down like rain and dew (32:2) \u8212? it comes toward the earth. At once heaven and earth are called as witnesses (32:1). Then the word Rock (tsur) keeps returning (32:4, 15, 18, 30, 31, 37): God is the Rock, the idols are not. The core of the song is a reversal: God found, carried and fed Israel (the eagle, 32:10\u8211?12), but \u8220?Jeshurun waxed fat and kicked\u8221? (32:15) and \u8220?forgot the Rock that begat him\u8221? (32:18). Abundance, not the wilderness, becomes the danger. Judgment follows \u8212? yet within God's faithfulness to His Name (32:26\u8211?27) \u8212? and the absolute claim \u8220?I kill and I make alive, I wound and I heal\u8221? (32:39). The ending: \u8220?no empty word, but your life\u8221? (32:47), and Moses himself must go up Nebo without entering (32:48\u8211?52).\par


\par\b\fs24 0.2 The four axes as an open question\b0\fs24\par


\par Does the weight fall on what God is and gives (Source: Rock, rain, dew, healing), on the movement through time (Trajectory: found \u8594? carried \u8594? fat \u8594? forgotten \u8594? judged \u8594? returned), on the wholeness of inner and outer (Coherence), or on the human response (Will: listen, return, rend your heart)? Do not decide yet.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.3 The centre of gravity read off\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? Load-bearing are \b Source\b0  and \b Trajectory\b0 : the unchanging Rock against a heart that, over time, forgets from whom it received everything. The chosen gospel (Matt. 18) draws a \b Coherence\b0  line through it: forgetting the Rock becomes visible in how you treat the other. Centre of gravity: the Rock who gives and heals, against the forgetting heart \u8212? and that forgetting or returning becomes visible in how one treats the neighbour.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.4 Framing test\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8908? Guarded. The weekly reflex lands on receiving/entrusting. Here the text does not land there: Ha'azinu is a history-in-verse (Trajectory) around a fixed Rock (Source), and the gospel choice Matt. 18 \u8212? the unforgiving servant \u8212? pushes toward Coherence (inner\u8596?outer), not toward surrender. Under guard stays the tendency to sentimentalise the ending into \u8220?letting go\u8221?; the song is sharp (judgment, ba'at \u8220?to kick\u8221?), and the teacher himself falls under the Word (Nebo).\par



\par\b\fs28 \u167?1 The thread\b0\fs24\par


\par The Word comes down like rain because heaven gives, not because the earth makes it. The Rock does not change; the heart does \u8212? especially in abundance, where receiving turns into possessing and the Giver is forgotten. Teshuvah is looking again at the One you left; and whoever returns and grasps how much has been forgiven him treats the other as one who, like him, lives from forgiveness.\par



